<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hong Kong (British)</h1><p class="st-meta">1841 CE – 1997 CE · capital Victoria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony on the south China coast that grew from a small island trading post into one of the world's great financial centres before its return to China in 1997.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>British forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> in January 1841 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanking</a> ceded it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1842. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> was added in 1860, and in 1898 the New Territories were leased for ninety-nine years, giving the colony its final shape within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor ruled with appointed councils, and the colony thrived as a free port and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied it from 1941 to 1945. After 1949 refugees from mainland China fuelled a manufacturing boom, and from the 1970s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> became a major financial centre. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murray-maclehose">Murray MacLehose</a> expanded housing and created the anti-corruption commission in 1974.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Because the New Territories lease expired in 1997, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-british-joint-declaration">Joint Declaration</a> in 1984. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chris-patten">Chris Patten</a> introduced late electoral reforms, and on 1 July 1997 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-administrative-regions-of-china">Special Administrative Region</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hong-kong-british.jpg" alt="Hong Kong (British)" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: College of Arms Vector: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hong_Kong_(1959%E2%80%931997).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1841 CE – 1997 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Victoria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry Pottinger, Murray MacLehose, Chris Patten</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cantonese, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Hong Kong, Colony of Hong Kong</dd></dl></aside></div>
